module j3.gfx.path
    SVG path data (`d="M 0 0 L 10 0 Q 10 10 0 10 Z"`) flattened to polylines, with no GL and no tree.
    Commands: `M L H V Q C S T A Z` and their lowercase (relative) forms.
    Curves become straight pieces; `parseFit` picks the
    finest curve split that fits the point budget the painter's shape shader can hold (`MAXPT`).
    Any thread: it only writes the slice you give it.
    ```
    let buf = mem.alloc::<f32>(2 * path.MAXPT);
    let p = path.parseFit("M2 2 H10 V10 H2 Z", buf);   // p.n points in buf, p.ok true
    ```

const MAXPT: int = 2048
    Points a shape can hold (and so a path fill).

const SEP_OPEN: f32 = -1000000000.0
    A point whose x is below `-1e8` ends a contour. `SEP_OPEN` ends a contour that was not closed
    with `Z` (a fill closes it anyway; a stroke does not), `SEP_CLOSED` one that was.

const SEP_CLOSED: f32 = -2000000000.0

struct Path
    A flattened path in `pts` (x, y pairs). `n` points are used; bounds cover the real points.
    pts: []f32
    n: int
    minx: f32
    miny: f32
    maxx: f32
    maxy: f32
    ok: bool
        False when the text had something unsupported or the buffer filled up (what was read stays).

fn isSep(x: f32): bool
    True for a contour separator point.
fn parse(d: string, out: []f32, perCurve: int): Path
    Parse `d` into `out` (room for `out.len / 2` points). `perCurve` is the pieces each curve gets.
fn parseFit(d: string, out: []f32): Path
    `parse` with the finest curve split (16, 8, 4, 2, 1 pieces) whose points all fit in `out`.
fn fillPoints(p: &Path, out: []f32, sx: f32, sy: f32, ox: f32, oy: f32): int
    Points of `p` mapped by `x * sx + ox`, `y * sy + oy` into `out` for a fill: every contour closed
    (open ones get their first point appended) and every separator `SEP_OPEN`. Returns the point
    count; contours that no longer fit are dropped whole.
fn numbers(s: string, out: []f32): int
    Up to `out.len` numbers from `s`, separated by spaces or commas (a `viewBox`); how many were read.
